@flexiarg problems/peripheral-hops-reset-self-regulation
@title Problem node: peripheral hops reset self-regulation
@keywords problem-node, peripheral, modeline, state, continuity
@audience agent-system designers, library authors
@tone plain
@style pattern
source-class: self-derived
@why committed pattern authority: library/gauntlet/modeline-persists-across-worlds.flexiarg:19
@how no mechanism claimed at problem grain; the source pattern names the persistent modeline

! conclusion: This is a problem-stating node: peripheral changes can discard an agent's dynamic self-regulation state.
  + context: Minted as a marked self-derived fallback after A5's external-first search found no project statement of this specific problem.
  + IF:
    An agent moves between peripheral modes.
  + HOWEVER:
    The problem, verbatim from the source pattern: “Each peripheral hop replaces the agent's world (P-1). Without a persistent surface, AIF state resets at each transition: the agent enters a new peripheral with no memory of how confident it was, how much budget remains, or what pattern it was carrying.” (source: futon3 library/gauntlet/modeline-persists-across-worlds.flexiarg:19)
  + THEN:
    Treat this node as the landing point for cross-peripheral state-continuity mechanisms.
  + BECAUSE:
    The source class makes the circular provenance mechanically visible.
